#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Film Part 1: Direction, Production Fundamentals, Cinematography -- pairs for upgrade_b"""
import json, pathlib

OUT = pathlib.Path('/workspace/data_upgrades/upgrade_b.jsonl')
OUT.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)

pairs = [
# ── FILM LANGUAGE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
("What is film language and how does a director use it intentionally?", "Film language is the vocabulary of visual and auditory choices that communicate meaning to an audience without words. It encompasses: shot size and framing (extreme close-up signals emotional intensity; wide shot establishes space and isolation); camera angle (low angle empowers subjects; high angle diminishes them; Dutch tilt creates unease); camera movement (dolly toward a character builds emotional intimacy; pull-back reveals wider context and can signal loss); lens choice (telephoto compresses space and creates surveillance feeling; wide lens distorts and exaggerates proximity); editing rhythm (fast cuts create tension and urgency; long takes demand patience and build dread or intimacy); sound design (silence amplifies terror; ambient sound grounds reality; score manipulates emotional state). A director uses these elements intentionally as a system: every choice should reinforce the scene's dramatic intention. The key question is always 'what do I want the audience to feel right now, and which combination of choices produces that feeling?' Intentional language means the camera becomes a character with a point of view -- not simply recording, but interpreting."),
("Explain the 180-degree rule and when a director should break it.", "The 180-degree rule states that two characters in a scene should always be filmed from the same side of an imaginary axis line drawn between them. This preserves consistent screen direction -- character A always appears on the left looking right; character B appears on the right looking left. Breaking the rule creates disorientation that registers subconsciously. When to maintain: all conventional scene coverage, dialogue scenes, chase sequences requiring consistent spatial orientation. When to break intentionally: Ozu broke it constantly to flatten spatial perspective and engage viewers with characters as subjects. Breaking mid-scene signals a shift in power dynamic or psychological state. In horror, crossing the line creates spatial confusion that amplifies dread. Rule of thumb: if the break is motivated by the scene's dramatic content and you can articulate why it serves the story, it is a directorial choice. If it confuses the audience without payoff, it is an error."),
("What is blocking and how does a director approach it?", "Blocking is the choreography of actors and camera within a scene -- the planned movement of bodies, objects, and the camera through space and time. Approach: (1) Read the scene for its essential dynamic -- who has power? Who wants what from whom? What changes by the end? The blocking should make those dynamics visible without stating them. (2) Begin with the geography -- walk the location or set; understand sightlines, natural movement paths, and constraints. (3) Work with actors first -- let actors instinctively move; good blocking often emerges from truthful impulse before the director shapes it. (4) Motivate all movement -- a character crosses toward the window because they cannot face the other person, not because the blocking chart says to cross. (5) Proximity is the most powerful tool -- moving two characters closer together or farther apart changes the scene's emotional temperature more than any other single choice. (6) Relate blocking to camera -- certain movements only work with certain camera positions; blocking for a 28mm lens in a small space differs entirely from a 100mm lens on a long street."),
("Explain the difference between shot types and when to use each.", "Shot types by frame size: (1) Extreme Wide Shot -- subject dwarfed by environment; establishes location, signals insignificance or isolation. (2) Wide Shot -- full body visible; establishes physical context; shows characters as small figures dominated by architecture or environment. (3) Medium Shot -- waist up; the most common coverage shot; conversational and neutral; easy to cut between. (4) Medium Close-Up -- chest to top of head; primary dialogue coverage in most film and TV; close enough for emotional nuance. (5) Close-Up -- face fills frame; for emotional peak moments, reactions, and revelations. (6) Extreme Close-Up -- one facial feature or a detail; creates intense intimacy or fetishises an object's significance. (7) Insert Shot -- an isolated detail (a hand picking up a key, a clock); provides specific information, gives the editor a cut option, focuses attention on significance. Selection logic: size of shot equals importance of information at that moment. Wide shots externalise experience; close-ups internalise it."),
("How does a director approach visual storytelling for a scene with no dialogue?", "Directing a dialogue-free scene forces pure cinema -- story told entirely through image, performance, and sound. Process: (1) Identify what the scene must communicate -- write it as a single declarative sentence. (2) Find the telling detail -- the single image that contains the scene's entire emotional meaning. (3) Choreograph performance for legibility -- without dialogue, the body is the text; posture, gaze direction, and physical relationship to objects and other characters carry the story. (4) Build a visual grammar -- a consistent logic of camera distance and movement that can break its own rules at the climactic moment. (5) Sound design as language -- ambient sound, music, or silence replaces dialogue; absence of speech heightens sensitivity to every other sound. (6) Editing rhythm -- without dialogue to anchor cuts, rhythm is purely emotional and intentional; a continuous take versus a mosaic of cuts each carries different meaning. Reference: the opening of Wall-E, the cave sequence in 2001, the wheat field in Gladiator -- pure visual storytelling without a spoken word."),

# ── SHOT COMPOSITION ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
("Explain compositional principles for film -- rule of thirds, depth, negative space.", "Key compositional principles: (1) Rule of thirds -- divide the frame into a 3x3 grid; place subjects on intersecting lines rather than dead centre; eyes of subjects fall on the upper horizontal third. Creates dynamic tension. (2) Leading lines -- natural or architectural lines that direct the eye toward the subject; roads, railings, corridors create depth. (3) Depth and layering -- foreground, midground, and background elements create dimensional space; shallow depth of field separates subject from environment; foreground layering adds meaning (a character framed through bars signals confinement). (4) Negative space -- emptiness is as meaningful as content; a figure isolated in negative space communicates loneliness or insignificance. (5) Symmetry -- balanced, symmetrical compositions create order and can signal control, beauty, or oppression; Kubrick's one-point perspective symmetry is his most recognisable signature. (6) Frame within a frame -- doorways, windows, mirrors, archways contain the subject within a secondary frame; adds depth and can signal surveillance or entrapment. (7) Headroom and look room -- appropriate space above the head; space in the direction the subject looks; looking into negative space creates openness; looking into the edge of frame creates tension."),
("What is the difference between a dolly move and a zoom?", "Dolly vs zoom: the fundamental spatial difference: (1) Dolly (physically moving the camera toward or away from the subject) -- changes the spatial relationship between camera and subject AND changes background perspective; creates genuine three-dimensional movement through space; the viewer experiences the space kinetically; dollying toward a character creates genuine intimacy because the audience is physically approaching. (2) Zoom (changing focal length while the camera stays stationary) -- magnifies the image but does not move through space; the background stays locked relative to the foreground; creates a flattening effect; feels like extraction rather than approach. The dolly zoom (Hitchcock/Vertigo effect): simultaneously dolly back and zoom in; subject stays the same size but background changes perspective dramatically, creating spatial dread. Zoom as style: 1960s-70s cinematography used the zoom extensively (Leone's rapid punch to the eyes in Spaghetti Westerns). Documentary zoom: responsive, naturalistic -- mimics the human experience of suddenly focusing attention. Default: dolly for scripted narrative; zoom for documentary aesthetic or specific period style."),
("Explain camera coverage and how a director plans it for efficiency on set.", "Camera coverage is the set of shots planned to capture a scene so the editor has sufficient material to cut it. Planning efficient coverage: (1) Start with the master shot -- a wide shot covering the entire scene from a single position; establishes geography; gives the editor a fallback. (2) Identify the minimum coverage set -- what is the absolute minimum to cut the scene? For a two-character dialogue scene: master + OTS for each character + individual CUs = four camera setups. (3) Prioritise moments, not angles -- which moment in the scene is most important dramatically? Ensure it has multiple shot options at different sizes. (4) Singles are the editor's currency -- individual close-up shots are what the editor will use most; captured in a second pass after the master. (5) Minimise flips -- coverage is typically shot in one direction first, then the camera flips to the other side; each flip takes time for lighting adjustment; minimise them under time pressure. (6) Communicate priorities to the 1st AD -- tell them which shots cannot be dropped if time runs short; protect the most critical coverage."),

# ── STORY STRUCTURE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
("How does story structure apply at the scene level, not just the screenplay level?", "Story structure from macro to scene level: Each scene should function as a micro-story with its own beginning (status quo entering the scene), middle (complication and conflict), and end (changed status quo). Three questions for every scene: (1) What is the scene's function in the larger story? If you can cut the scene without losing its function, cut it. (2) What is the entry and exit emotional state of each character? The emotional journey within a scene should be earned. (3) What is the most dramatically active version of this scene? Scenes that begin later in the action and end before they resolve create forward momentum. The screenwriting rule: enter late, leave early. Story structure at scene level also governs camera: the camera position at the start of a scene (before conflict) can be neutral; by the scene's climax, it should be the most dramatically charged choice in your repertoire. The scene's beat map (moments where the power or objective shifts) determines where you need coverage options most."),
("What are character arcs and how does a director protect them across a shoot?", "A character arc is the internal transformation a character undergoes over the course of the story -- the journey from one state of being to another. Types: transformative arc (character fundamentally changes: learns to love, loses innocence, finds courage); flat arc (character remains constant but changes the world around them: the moral anchor who forces others to reckon with their values); negative arc (character degrades or fails to grow: tragedy). Protecting arcs across a non-linear shoot: (1) Character map -- for each major character, map their emotional state scene-by-scene on a single document; the director carries this as a reference throughout production. (2) Continuity of internal state -- when shooting scene 47 of the script as day 3 of the shoot, the director must know where the character is emotionally in the arc at that point, regardless of what was shot yesterday. (3) Actor conversations -- brief the actor on arc context before each shooting day; they should know their character's emotional history up to this scene in story order, not shoot order. (4) Protecting the reveal -- scenes that pay off an arc turn require specific coverage choices (the close-up that shows the change in the character's eyes must be in the shot list). (5) Editorial protection -- if arc continuity is damaged by a missed moment in shooting, the editor needs to know so they can find an alternative in coverage or flag the problem for pickup."),
("Explain tone and mood design in film direction.", "Tone is the overall emotional register of the film -- its attitude toward its subject matter and audience. Mood is the emotional atmosphere of a specific scene or sequence. They operate at different scales but must be consistent. Tone design begins in pre-production: the director and DP create a visual bible (lookbook of reference images, films, paintings, photographs) that establishes the visual tone. Key elements of tone and mood: (1) Lighting -- high-key lighting (bright, low contrast) creates comedy, romance, optimism; low-key lighting (shadows, high contrast) creates drama, noir, threat. The ratio of shadow to highlight is one of the most powerful mood controls available. (2) Colour palette -- warm colours (amber, red) create intimacy and danger; cool colours (blue, grey) create detachment and melancholy; desaturated palette creates naturalism; heightened saturation creates fantasy or stylisation. (3) Camera movement -- smooth, controlled movement creates elegance and control; handheld creates intimacy, anxiety, or chaos. (4) Pacing -- slow takes and long pauses create contemplative or ominous tone; rapid cuts and short takes create urgency. (5) Casting -- the actor's natural persona contributes to tone; casting against type creates tonal complexity. (6) Music -- the most direct emotional manipulation tool; using silence or diegetic sound instead of score creates naturalism. Consistency of tone is a director's primary responsibility to the audience's experience."),
("How do genre conventions serve and constrain a director?", "Genre conventions are the shared expectations audiences bring to specific genre labels. They serve directors as: (1) Communication shorthand -- audiences arrive pre-loaded with emotional expectations; a horror film's first act can establish dread faster because the audience already expects it. (2) A contract -- genre sets up promises that the film must pay off; the thriller promises a revelation; the comedy promises release; the romance promises connection. (3) A grammar -- genre gives the director a tested vocabulary; the horror close-up of an open door has decades of connotation the director inherits for free. Constraints: (1) Originality risk -- pure genre execution can feel mechanical; audiences who are sophisticated genre consumers (horror fans, thriller readers) become immune to conventions. (2) Tonal inconsistency -- mixing genre conventions carelessly produces confusion; a horror film that suddenly becomes a comedy must earn that shift through careful tonal preparation. (3) Commercial pressure -- studios often demand genre adherence that limits directorial vision. Strategies for using genre intelligently: (a) Subversion -- honour enough conventions to establish the genre contract, then break specific conventions at the moment of highest expectation (Get Out uses horror conventions but the threat comes from a direction the genre has not trained audiences to fear). (b) Elevation -- execute the genre with exceptional craft, depth, and specificity (Moonlight is a coming-of-age film elevated by its cinematographic and emotional precision). (c) Hybridisation -- combine genre conventions deliberately (Parasite: social drama + thriller + horror + comedy, each emerging as the film's tone evolves)."),

# ── PRE-PRODUCTION, PRODUCTION, POST-PRODUCTION ───────────────────────────────
("Walk through the pre-production process for a feature film.", "Pre-production is everything that happens between greenlight and first day of principal photography. Timeline and phases: (1) Script lock -- final shooting script approved; all departments work from this version; script changes during pre-production are formal (colour-coded revision pages). (2) Crewing -- HoDs hired in priority order: DP, production designer, 1st AD, costume designer, casting director; department heads then hire their crews. (3) Scheduling -- 1st AD breaks the script into shooting strips and builds the schedule (Movie Magic Scheduling); factors: location availability, actor availability (star schedules drive the schedule), company moves, equipment constraints; target: fewest company moves, most efficient use of each location and actor. (4) Budgeting -- line producer or UPM (Unit Production Manager) builds the budget from the schedule; each department submits a budget; total reconciled to the production's approved budget; overages identified and value-engineered. (5) Location scouting -- location manager scoutes and scouts; director and DP approve locations; tech scouts (department heads walk each location with measuring tape and camera); logistics (permits, power, parking, catering). (6) Design and art direction -- production designer creates the visual world in collaboration with the director; set construction, set dressing, prop sourcing; all approved against the visual bible. (7) Casting -- casting director submits shortlists; director holds auditions; offers made through agents; contracts negotiated. (8) Camera tests -- DP and director test lens packages, film stocks or camera settings, VFX elements, and makeup under the intended lighting conditions. (9) Rehearsals -- table read, blocking rehearsals, actor work sessions. (10) Pre-vis -- VFX-heavy sequences storyboarded and pre-visualised in 3D to guide all departments."),
("What is a call sheet and what information does it contain?", "A call sheet is the daily production bible -- the official document issued the night before each shooting day that tells every member of cast and crew where to be, when to be there, and what will be shot. Standard call sheet contents: (1) Header -- production company, project title, shooting day number (of total), date, general call time (when the crew generally arrives), nearest hospital address. (2) Location information -- address of primary location and any secondary locations; parking coordinates; nearest cross-street; map link. (3) Weather forecast -- essential for outdoor shoots; affects contingency plans. (4) Advanced schedule -- scenes being shot today in shooting order; for each scene: scene number, scene description, cast involved, interior/exterior, day/night, page count. (5) Cast call times -- each cast member listed with: character name, makeup call, hair call, wardrobe call, set call (when they're needed on set); different actors have different prep times. (6) Crew calls -- department-by-department call times; grip and electric often arrive earliest to prep the first lighting setup. (7) Equipment -- camera, grip, electric equipment listed; trucks and facilities listed. (8) Extras -- number of background actors, their general call and wardrobe instructions. (9) Production notes -- any special instructions, unit moves, specific equipment or props needed. (10) Tomorrow's schedule -- advance notice of the following day's work so departments can prep. The 1st AD builds the call sheet; the UPM approves it; it is distributed by late evening of the day before. Changes issued as revised call sheets with timestamps."),
("Explain the post-production workflow for a feature film.", "Post-production workflow: (1) Picture editorial -- the picture editor begins cutting during production (receiving dailies); assembly cut (all selected footage in story order, typically 2-3x the intended length); rough cut (director and editor collaboration, major structural decisions); fine cut (scene-by-scene refinement); director's cut (director's final vision before studio feedback); locked picture (no more picture changes; triggers all other post processes). (2) Visual effects -- VFX supervisor works from the locked picture; shots composited, tracked, and rendered; temp VFX replaced with finals; often continues until weeks before release. (3) Music -- composer writes score to picture lock (or to fine cut if schedule demands); temp score guides emotional beats; final score recorded with live orchestra or produced electronically; songs licensed separately. (4) Sound editorial -- dialogue editing (selecting cleanest production takes, removing noise); ADR (Automated Dialogue Replacement -- actors re-record specific lines in a studio); Foley (recording specific sound effects to picture: footsteps, cloth, impacts); sound effects editing. (5) Sound mix -- all audio elements (dialogue, music, effects, ambience) mixed in a dubbing stage; 5.1, 7.1, Dolby Atmos formats; dialogue mix (intelligibility), music mix (emotional support without overwhelming dialogue), effects mix. (6) Colour grading -- colourist works with the DP on a calibrated display; primary correction (exposure, colour balance); secondary correction (selective adjustments to skin tone, sky, specific objects); look creation (applying the intended visual tone). (7) Delivery -- master files delivered in DCP (Digital Cinema Package) for theatrical; IMF packages for streaming platforms."),
("How does scheduling work and what drives the order of a shooting schedule?", "Scheduling logic -- the 1st AD's core discipline: (1) Actor availability -- A-list cast schedules and contract dates drive the entire schedule; most expensive actors scheduled first and concentrated to minimise their days (shooting days = billing days = cost). (2) Location availability -- permits, weather, season, location owner restrictions narrow the window for each location; interior locations (stages) are most flexible; exterior locations are weather-dependent. (3) Company moves -- moving the entire production from one location to another costs half a day in travel and setup; minimise moves by shooting all scenes at each location consecutively regardless of story order. (4) Day/night splits -- shooting at night is more expensive (night differential pay) and limits the crew's shooting hours (turnaround requirements); group all night exteriors at one location on consecutive nights. (5) Child actors -- legal working hour limits for minors restrict their availability to 4-6 hours/day depending on jurisdiction; schedule their scenes in compressed blocks. (6) Special effects and stunts -- VFX elements, pyrotechnics, and stunt sequences require prep time and safety inspections; often scheduled for a dedicated day or end of a shooting block. (7) Weather cover -- an interior scene ready to shoot on any day in case outdoor conditions make exterior shooting impossible. (8) Page count target -- a typical feature film production targets 3-5 pages per day; complex action or VFX scenes might do 1 page/day; simpler dialogue scenes might reach 8-10 pages/day. (9) Sequence logic -- shooting in story order helps actors' continuity but is rarely possible or economical; emotional continuity for actors is protected through daily briefings and arc tracking."),

# ── SHOT LIST AND STORYBOARD ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
("How do you create a shot list as a director?", "Shot list creation process: A shot list is the director's translation of the visual concept into specific camera instructions for the DP and crew. Structure: each line item includes -- (1) Scene number (from the script); (2) Shot number (sequential within the scene: 1, 2, 3...); (3) Shot size/type (WS, MS, MCU, CU, ECU, Insert); (4) Lens (if specified: 35mm, 50mm, 85mm); (5) Camera position (relative to the scene: front, 3/4 profile, OTS, POV); (6) Camera movement (static, pan left, tilt up, dolly in, crane up, handheld); (7) Subject/description (what is in the frame: 'SARAH at the window, looking out at the rain'); (8) Notes (any special instruction: 'motivate with her turning', 'rack focus from the clock to her face'). Best practices: (1) Build the list from the scene breakdown, not in isolation; every shot should serve a specific narrative or emotional function. (2) Sequence shots by camera position -- group all shots from one camera setup consecutively even if they appear at different story moments. (3) Mark the non-negotiable shots -- star or bold the shots that are essential; these cannot be dropped if time runs out. (4) Build in contingency -- note which shots are 'nice to have' vs essential; a realistic schedule is not over-covered. (5) Share and discuss with the DP before the shoot -- the DP may suggest modifications based on their lighting plan or equipment; collaboration produces better results than unilateral prescription."),
("What is storyboard logic and when should a director invest in storyboards?", "Storyboard logic -- storyboards translate the shot list into visual frames, showing composition, camera angle, lens choice, and movement for each planned shot. When to invest: (1) Complex action sequences -- car chases, fight choreography, VFX-heavy sequences; without boards, coordinating the camera with stunt or VFX departments on set is prohibitively slow and expensive. (2) VFX pre-visualisation -- any shot that requires elements created in post (CGI characters, set extensions, invisible effects) must be pre-visualised so VFX can plan their pipeline; the board communicates the frame precisely. (3) Complex crane or gimbal moves -- elaborate camera choreography requires the rigging team to pre-plan their equipment; boards give them the reference. (4) Shots that must work in sequence -- if five shots must cut together in a specific rhythm, boards ensure each individual shot is composed to serve the intended sequence. When not to invest: (1) Dialogue scenes -- boards can harm organic actor-director collaboration; blocking evolves from rehearsal and should not be locked in pre-production. (2) Naturalistic or documentary-style work -- predetermined compositions conflict with the handheld, responsive aesthetic. (3) Limited budget productions where a clear shot list serves the same function. Board alternatives: animatics (simple animation of the storyboard) for VFX-heavy sequences; pre-vis (full 3D animated preview of a sequence). The director must be able to articulate every shot whether or not it is drawn."),
("Explain director's notes organisation and how to use them effectively on set.", "Director's notes are the accumulating documentation of directorial intent across the entire production. Categories: (1) Scene notes -- specific intentions for each scene: emotional temperature, key beats, specific physical or verbal moments to capture, performance notes from the writer's intent; kept in a scene-by-scene binder. (2) Character notes -- per-character arc tracking, specific notes on actor's interpretation and adjustments, dialogue that must be preserved vs dialogue that can be improvised. (3) Performance notes from rehearsal -- what worked in rehearsal that should be protected on set; moments of unexpected discovery that must be replicated. (4) Visual notes -- sketches, reference images, specific framing ideas; organised by scene. (5) Daily notes from set -- moments that were not captured as planned; pickups needed; mistakes or compromises that need editorial flags. Organisation system: a physical script binder with tabs per scene, containing scene notes, shot list, reference images, and coverage diagram; the director carries this at all times on set. Digital alternative: a custom Notion or Airtable database per project. Notes must be externalised -- a director cannot hold all of this in working memory across a 50-day shoot; the notes are the memory. Communication: share relevant notes with the 1st AD (impacts scheduling), the DP (impacts technical decisions), and the editor (impacts their interpretation of available material)."),
("How do you plan coverage for a scene at a specific location?", "Location-specific coverage planning: (1) Tech scout the location thoroughly -- walk it with the DP; identify: available light sources and their direction throughout the day; practical obstacles (low ceilings, narrow spaces, structural columns); power access points; ambient sound issues (traffic, HVAC, water); sightlines from every potential camera position. (2) Determine the primary axis -- where will the key dramatic action occur? The primary axis determines the 180-degree line. (3) Map the sightlines for each planned shot -- can the 50mm lens at position A capture the composition needed without showing crew? Does the ceiling height allow a high-angle shot? Is there room for a dolly track? (4) Plan lighting zones -- which areas will be lit? From where? Identify practical lights that can serve as motivated sources. (5) Consider the time-of-day constraint -- exterior locations have a specific window of flattering light (golden hour, open shade, magic hour); schedule the most visually critical shots within that window. (6) Adapt the shot list to the location's reality -- the ideal composition may not be achievable in the actual space; modify the plan based on physical constraints discovered during the tech scout. (7) Identify hidden assets -- a location often has unexpected visual opportunities that improve on the scripted plan; bring these to the director's attention (a beautiful reflection, an interesting architectural element, a practical light that creates a perfect effect). (8) Plan for contingency -- if the primary setup is delayed by a technical problem, what is the next shot that can be executed with the current lighting and equipment position?"),

# ── CINEMATOGRAPHY ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
("Explain lenses and focal lengths in cinematography -- what each does to the image.", "Lens focal length governs two related things: angle of view (how much of the scene is captured) and perspective compression (how it represents depth). Key focal lengths: (1) Ultra-wide (14-24mm) -- extreme angle of view; distorts faces and architecture at close range; creates dramatic spatial exaggeration; used for immersive environments, subjective claustrophobia, or comedic distortion. (2) Wide (24-35mm) -- covers a large area; slightly exaggerated depth perception; creates a sense of environment around the character; feels immediate and real; favoured by documentary cinematographers and naturalistic directors (Malick, the Dardenne brothers). (3) Normal (40-58mm) -- closest to human eye perspective; minimal distortion; neutral spatial representation; the 50mm is historically the 'invisible' lens -- does not call attention to itself. (4) Short telephoto (75-100mm) -- moderate compression; flattering to faces (slightly compresses features without distortion); most used portrait focal length; creates some separation between subject and background. (5) Telephoto (135-200mm+) -- strong background compression; subjects appear close to background elements even when physically separated; creates a surveillance or observed feeling; beautiful bokeh at wide apertures; used for intimacy from a distance (street photography aesthetic, naturalistic performance capture). (6) Anamorphic lenses -- wider aspect ratio capture (2.39:1 on a sensor meant for 1.78:1); distinctive horizontal lens flare; oval bokeh; breathing on focus pulls; associated with cinematic scale and specific visual prestige."),
("What are the fundamentals of cinematic lighting?", "Cinematic lighting fundamentals: (1) Three-point lighting (foundation): Key light (primary source; establishes direction and main shadow); Fill light (reduces shadow intensity on the key-side's opposite; controls contrast ratio; lower = more dramatic shadow); Back/hair light (separates subject from background; adds depth). (2) Contrast ratio -- the ratio of light to shadow on the subject's face; high ratio (1:8 or 1:16) = dramatic, noir, thriller; low ratio (1:2) = flat, documentary, commercial. (3) Light quality -- hard light (direct source: sun, bare bulb; creates sharp, defined shadows; used for dramatic, textured looks); soft light (diffused source: bounced light, diffusion fabric, overcast sky; wraps around the subject; reduces shadows; used for beauty, intimacy, naturalism). (4) Colour temperature -- warm light (2700-3200K: tungsten, candlelight, amber gels) creates intimacy, nostalgia, warmth; cool light (5600-6500K: daylight, HMI, blue gels) creates clinical, cold, alienated feel; mixing temperatures creates contrast and visual complexity. (5) Motivated lighting -- every light source in a scene should appear to come from a practical source visible in the frame or implied by the scene's geography (window, desk lamp, street light). Unmotivated lighting undermines visual credibility. (6) Exposure -- iris (aperture) controls depth of field; shutter speed controls motion blur (180-degree rule: shutter speed = double the frame rate; 24fps = 1/48 shutter); ISO controls sensor sensitivity and noise. (7) Look development -- combining colour temperature choices, contrast ratio, and practical sources to create a consistent visual identity."),
("How does camera movement affect the emotional experience of a scene?", "Camera movement creates kinetic meaning -- the camera is not a passive recorder but an active participant in the emotional experience: (1) Static camera -- stillness implies objectivity, observation, weight; Kubrick's still wide shots create a god-like detachment; Haneke's static camera refuses to comfort the viewer; in moments of high drama, a static camera can be more powerful than any movement. (2) Pan and tilt -- horizontal and vertical pivoting of the camera from a fixed point; follows action or reveals information; a slow pan can be meditative or suspenseful depending on context. (3) Dolly/truck -- physically moving the camera on a track parallel to the action (truck) or toward/away from the subject (dolly in/out); movement through space; the dolly in creates intimacy through physical approach; the dolly out reveals context and can create revelation or alienation. (4) Crane/jib -- vertical movement or sweeping arcs; the crane up creates a sense of departure or transcendence; a crane rising to reveal a landscape expands the world. (5) Handheld -- organic, intimate, anxious, or chaotic depending on intensity; low-amplitude handheld mimics the barely perceptible movement of a person watching attentively; high-amplitude handheld signals chaos or urgency. (6) Steadicam -- smooth floating movement that follows characters through space; creates a sense of the camera as a consciousness accompanying the character (The Shining's tracking shots through the Overlook create a pursuing, predatory feeling). (7) Speed ramping -- slow motion stretches a moment's emotional weight (a punch, a reaction, a natural event); fast motion compresses time and can create comedy or urgency."),
("Explain aspect ratios and their visual and emotional implications.", "Aspect ratio is the relationship of frame width to height and has significant visual and emotional implications: (1) 4:3 (1.33:1) -- the classic film and television ratio; squarish frame; creates a sense of intimacy, classicism, and constraint; used by contemporary filmmakers for specific effect: The Lighthouse (Eggers), First Reformed (Schrader), Beau Travail; the square frame emphasises height and face, creates a sense of claustrophobia or timelessness. (2) 16:9 (1.78:1) -- contemporary television standard; comfortable widescreen that balances intimacy and landscape without committing to extreme widescreen. (3) 1.85:1 -- the standard theatrical 'flat' widescreen; slightly wider than 16:9; most comfortable composition for single characters and dialogue scenes; dominant American film format. (4) 2.39:1 anamorphic scope -- the extreme widescreen format; creates a sense of cinematic scale, landscape, and visual grandeur; requires compositional discipline (empty space on the sides of a face feels different at 2.39:1 than at 1.85:1); the letterbox format historically associated with epic cinema. (5) Variable ratio -- some films change aspect ratio within the film to signal tonal or temporal shifts: The Grand Budapest Hotel uses three different ratios to represent three different time periods. Implication for composition: a narrower ratio directs attention to the vertical plane (characters' height, their relationship to ceilings, sky, ground); a wider ratio emphasises the horizontal plane (landscape, physical distance between characters, groups)."),
("How does visual continuity work and what breaks it?", "Visual continuity is the maintenance of consistent spatial, temporal, and physical details across all shots in a scene so the audience perceives the scene as a continuous, coherent reality. Elements: (1) Screen direction -- established by the 180-degree rule; a character moving right in one shot should continue moving right in the next; violating creates spatial confusion. (2) Eyeline match -- if character A looks left in a close-up, character B (to whom A is looking) should appear on the right in the next shot and be looking slightly right (toward A's position); eyeline mismatch breaks the spatial logic. (3) Match on action -- cutting on a physical action in progress (a hand reaching for a glass); both shots should show the action at the same point in motion; mismatched action is the most jarring continuity error. (4) Physical continuity -- props, wardrobe, hair, and set dressing must match exactly between shots filmed hours or days apart; the script supervisor tracks all of this with a continuity log and photographs. A glass half-full in the master cannot be full in the close-up. (5) Lighting continuity -- the quality and direction of light should not shift inexplicably between shots in the same scene; weather changes, time of day shifts during exterior shooting, or lamp position changes all create lighting discontinuity. (6) Sound continuity -- ambient sound should not change abruptly between shots in the same location; production sound mixer records room tone for every location so the editor can fill gaps. Intentional violations: directors sometimes violate continuity rules deliberately to create disorientation or signal temporal discontinuity (Resnais in Hiroshima Mon Amour; Godard throughout)."),
("Explain look development and mood boards for a film's visual identity.", "Look development is the pre-production process of defining the film's visual aesthetic -- establishing the visual language that all departments will use as a reference throughout production. Process: (1) Reference gathering -- the director and DP collect reference images: paintings (Rembrandt lighting for a period drama, Edward Hopper's isolation for an American suburban story), photographs (Vivian Maier's street texture, Gregory Crewdson's staged suburbia), and frames from other films (not to copy but to communicate visual intention). (2) Mood board construction -- references are organised into a visual document (often in a booklet or presentation); arranged by: lighting style, colour palette, camera distance preferences, texture (film grain, digital clarity), costume and production design aesthetic. (3) Camera and lens tests -- the DP tests different lenses, camera settings, film stocks or digital profiles, filtration (diffusion, colour correction filters) to find the combination that produces the intended look. (4) Grade preview -- an early grade (colour grading) test on test footage shows how the intended look will be realised in post. (5) Communication to departments -- the mood board is the visual bible shared with: production designer (set colours and textures), costume designer (palette and texture of wardrobe), hair and makeup (skin tone, period style, naturalism vs stylisation). (6) Consistency across the shoot -- the mood board prevents individual decisions by individual departments from creating visual incoherence; every choice is evaluated against the reference. Look development for different visual identities: The Revenant (Lubezki) = natural light only, extreme wide lenses; Blade Runner 2049 (Deakins) = industrial amber vs cool digital blue opposition; Moonlight (Laxton) = saturated blues and golds that shift emotional temperature by act."),
("Explain how a DP and director collaborate on look development.", "DP-director collaboration on visual language: the relationship between director and cinematographer is the most important creative partnership in film production. Process: (1) Concept alignment -- the director communicates the film's emotional and thematic intention; the DP proposes visual strategies to serve those intentions; both parties must understand and commit to the same underlying concept, not just the surface aesthetic. (2) Reference sharing -- both parties bring visual references; comparing interpretations of references reveals alignment and misalignment in taste and intention. (3) Location scouting together -- the DP sees locations as lighting and composition problems; the director sees them as dramatic opportunities; together they negotiate which locations serve both needs. (4) Camera package decision -- the DP proposes camera body and lens package based on the look developed; the director weighs in on optical qualities that affect the intended feel. (5) Lighting philosophy agreement -- key questions: How motivated do practical sources need to be? How much ambient light vs controlled light? Will we use natural light as a primary source (requiring location and schedule flexibility) or fully control all light? How does the lighting change as the film's tone shifts (e.g., act 1 is warm, act 3 is cold)? (6) Shot list collaboration -- the director presents the shot list; the DP advises on practical execution; together they simplify the list to what is achievable in the available time without sacrificing dramatic necessity. (7) Daily review of dailies -- director and DP watch each day's footage together and make real-time adjustments to lighting, lens choice, and coverage strategy."),

# ── PRODUCTION WORKFLOW ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
("Explain the production workflow from day one of principal photography to picture lock.", "Principal photography to picture lock: (1) Daily production rhythm -- 5-6 AM: crew arrives; grip and electric prep the first setup; camera department preps equipment. 7-8 AM: set lighting, camera position established by the DP and gaffer. 9 AM: first shot of the day ('first team in'). Set rhythm: each shot follows -- rehearse with stand-ins, light, first team (actors) rehearse on set, final adjustments, shoot 2-5 takes per setup, move to next setup. End of day: equipment secured, locations notes filed, call sheet for the next day distributed. (2) Dailies -- footage from each day is reviewed (traditionally in a screening room, now typically via a secure digital dailies platform: Dailies, Pix, Frame.io); director, DP, editor, and key producers watch; selects (circled takes) communicated to the editor. (3) Cutting during production -- the editor begins cutting as soon as footage arrives; builds assembly sequences; the director reviews periodically during production to catch performance or coverage issues that can be addressed in an upcoming reshoot or pickup day. (4) End of principal photography ('wrap') -- all scheduled production complete; all assets (camera, grip, electric, vehicles) returned. (5) Post-production begins -- editor has assembly cut; director returns from production and begins picture editorial collaboration. (6) Stages of edit -- assembly (all selects in order) -> rough cut (structure working) -> fine cut (scene-level precision) -> director's cut -> studio/executive notes cut -> locked picture. Timeline: a feature film typically takes 6-12 months from photography to locked picture."),
("How does a director use rehearsal effectively?", "Rehearsal strategy for film directors: Film rehearsal is different from theatre rehearsal -- the goal is not a polished, repeatable performance but rather discovery of the scene's truth and technical preparation for efficient shooting. Approaches: (1) Table read -- the first collective read of the script; reveals rhythm, clarity, and actor interpretation; the director listens without interruption initially; then guides discussion of character intention and scene dynamics without prescribing choices. (2) Character work sessions -- individual or paired sessions with actors to explore backstory, relationship history, and character psychology; these are exploration sessions, not blocking sessions; the director facilitates rather than instructs. (3) Space rehearsals (blocking rehearsal) -- with the script in hand, work through the blocking for key sequences; camera positions not yet established; focus on organic movement and physical relationships. (4) Camera rehearsal (day of shooting) -- the first rehearsal on set with the actual camera position; actors use their blocking; the DP sets lights; the focus puller marks distances. This is not the creative rehearsal -- it is technical confirmation. (5) What to protect from rehearsal -- discoveries made in the rehearsal room (an unexpected moment of vulnerability, a physical gesture that reveals character) must be communicated to the DP so they are protected with appropriate coverage. (6) When NOT to over-rehearse -- spontaneity is often more valuable than precision; too much rehearsal can make performances 'correct' but dead; first-take energy for emotionally complex scenes is often irreplaceable."),
]

